<h2>Why the trade-off exists</h2>

<p>Sheen comes from the ratio of pigment to binder. A flat paint carries a lot
of pigment and relatively little binder, which leaves a microscopically rough
surface that scatters light in every direction &mdash; so a dent casts no
consistent highlight and disappears. A gloss paint is the reverse: more binder,
a smoother film, light reflecting in one direction.</p>

<p>That same binder is what makes the film tough. It is what resists a sponge,
a scuff and a child's hand. So the property that makes gloss durable is
inseparable from the property that makes it unforgiving about a wall that is not
perfectly flat. You cannot buy your way out of it; you can only pick which
problem you would rather have.</p>

