<h2>Room by room</h2>

<p><strong>Ceilings</strong> take flat almost without exception. They are
rarely touched, they are lit at a raking angle that exposes every imperfection,
and flat is the only sheen that forgives that.</p>

<p><strong>Living and dining rooms</strong> generally want eggshell. Enough
washability for occasional marks, enough flatness to keep an older wall looking
composed.</p>

<p><strong>Hallways, stairwells and kids' rooms</strong> earn satin. These are
the surfaces that get hands, bags and bikes against them, and the extra
scrubbability is worth the extra visible texture.</p>

<p><strong>Kitchens and bathrooms</strong> want satin or semi-gloss, less for
the scrubbing than for the moisture. A tighter film sheds condensation instead
of absorbing it, which is what keeps mildew from taking hold in a grout line's
worth of damp.</p>

<p><strong>Trim, doors and cabinets</strong> are semi-gloss or gloss almost
always. They get touched constantly, they are usually a different colour from
the wall anyway, and the sheen change is part of how trim reads as trim.</p>

<h2>Two things people get wrong</h2>

<p><strong>Sheen is not consistent between brands.</strong> One manufacturer's
eggshell can be noticeably glossier than another's satin. If you are touching up
or matching an adjacent wall, the brand and line matter as much as the sheen
name on the can.</p>

<p><strong>Sheen changes the apparent colour.</strong> The same tint reads
lighter and slightly washed out in gloss, because you are seeing reflected room
light as well as pigment. If you picked a colour off a flat chip and are
painting in semi-gloss, expect it to look a shade different.</p>

